WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.121
Listening and Asking Questions.

2
00:00:02.401 --> 00:00:06.859
Day 17 of the Soft Skill Playbook course, in the Communication phase.

3
00:00:08.609 --> 00:00:09.633
The key idea.

4
00:00:09.913 --> 00:00:13.348
Good listeners ask good questions, and people trust them more.

5
00:00:13.628 --> 00:00:16.423
Listening is the most underrated skill on this site.

6
00:00:17.773 --> 00:00:18.626
Part 1.

7
00:00:18.906 --> 00:00:20.698
Active listening in 4 moves.

8
00:00:21.948 --> 00:00:22.865
Step 1:

9
00:00:23.145 --> 00:00:27.113
Pay attention: close the laptop, look at them, don't plan your reply.

10
00:00:27.393 --> 00:00:28.311
Step 2:

11
00:00:28.591 --> 00:00:29.487
Acknowledge:

12
00:00:29.767 --> 00:00:32.028
Okay, I see, a nod; show you're with them.

13
00:00:32.308 --> 00:00:33.225
Step 3:

14
00:00:33.505 --> 00:00:34.508
Paraphrase:

15
00:00:34.788 --> 00:00:36.111
So what you're saying is...

16
00:00:36.391 --> 00:00:37.351
Step 4:

17
00:00:37.631 --> 00:00:38.569
Clarify:

18
00:00:38.849 --> 00:00:41.623
When you say 'urgent', do you mean today or this week?

19
00:00:42.973 --> 00:00:43.847
Example:

20
00:00:44.127 --> 00:00:45.557
Paraphrase in practice.

21
00:00:45.837 --> 00:00:46.733
Manager:

22
00:00:47.013 --> 00:00:51.599
The client is unhappy, the dashboard keeps breaking and nobody tells them anything.

23
00:00:51.879 --> 00:00:52.519
You:

24
00:00:52.799 --> 00:00:58.069
So there are two problems: the dashboard's reliability, and how we communicate when it breaks.

25
00:00:58.349 --> 00:01:00.141
Which one hurts them more right now?

26
00:01:01.491 --> 00:01:02.365
Part 2.

27
00:01:02.645 --> 00:01:04.821
Question types and when to use them.

28
00:01:06.071 --> 00:01:07.437
Here is a quick table:

29
00:01:07.717 --> 00:01:09.658
Type, and Example, and Use when.

30
00:01:09.938 --> 00:01:10.706
Open.

31
00:01:10.986 --> 00:01:11.861
Example:

32
00:01:12.141 --> 00:01:13.634
How do you see this working?

33
00:01:13.914 --> 00:01:14.789
Use when:

34
00:01:15.069 --> 00:01:16.797
Exploring, building rapport.

35
00:01:17.077 --> 00:01:17.887
Closed.

36
00:01:18.167 --> 00:01:19.042
Example:

37
00:01:19.322 --> 00:01:20.773
Is the deadline Friday?

38
00:01:21.053 --> 00:01:21.927
Use when:

39
00:01:22.207 --> 00:01:23.381
Confirming a fact.

40
00:01:23.661 --> 00:01:24.663
Clarifying.

41
00:01:24.943 --> 00:01:25.818
Example:

42
00:01:26.098 --> 00:01:27.805
What does 'done' mean for this ticket?

43
00:01:28.085 --> 00:01:28.959
Use when:

44
00:01:29.239 --> 00:01:30.583
Requirements are vague.

45
00:01:30.863 --> 00:01:31.674
Probing.

46
00:01:31.954 --> 00:01:32.829
Example:

47
00:01:33.109 --> 00:01:34.410
What makes you say that?

48
00:01:34.690 --> 00:01:35.565
Use when:

49
00:01:35.845 --> 00:01:37.338
Getting to the real reason.

50
00:01:37.618 --> 00:01:38.706
Hypothetical.

51
00:01:38.986 --> 00:01:39.861
Example:

52
00:01:40.141 --> 00:01:42.359
What if we shipped without the export feature?

53
00:01:42.639 --> 00:01:43.514
Use when:

54
00:01:43.794 --> 00:01:45.117
Negotiating scope.

55
00:01:45.397 --> 00:01:46.357
Reflective.

56
00:01:46.637 --> 00:01:47.511
Example:

57
00:01:47.791 --> 00:01:50.053
It sounds like you're frustrated with the delays?

58
00:01:50.333 --> 00:01:51.207
Use when:

59
00:01:51.487 --> 00:01:52.661
Emotions are high.

60
00:01:52.941 --> 00:01:53.709
Summary.

61
00:01:53.989 --> 00:01:54.863
Example:

62
00:01:55.143 --> 00:01:57.106
So we agreed X, Y, Z, right?

63
00:01:57.386 --> 00:01:58.261
Use when:

64
00:01:58.541 --> 00:02:00.013
Closing a conversation.

65
00:02:01.363 --> 00:02:02.259
Part 3.

66
00:02:02.539 --> 00:02:04.672
Questions every developer should ask.

67
00:02:05.922 --> 00:02:07.351
Before starting a task.

68
00:02:07.631 --> 00:02:09.871
What problem are we solving, and for whom?

69
00:02:10.151 --> 00:02:11.453
What does done look like?

70
00:02:11.733 --> 00:02:12.991
How will we test it?

71
00:02:13.271 --> 00:02:16.087
What is the deadline, and is it fixed or flexible?

72
00:02:16.367 --> 00:02:17.733
Who else is affected?

73
00:02:18.013 --> 00:02:19.122
Who should review it?

74
00:02:19.402 --> 00:02:21.962
Is there anything you've already tried or decided?

75
00:02:23.312 --> 00:02:24.251
When you're stuck.

76
00:02:24.531 --> 00:02:25.747
I've tried A and B.

77
00:02:26.027 --> 00:02:28.501
Before I go further, am I on the right track?

78
00:02:28.781 --> 00:02:30.744
Who knows the most about this module?

79
00:02:31.024 --> 00:02:34.096
Can you point me to an example of how we usually do this?

80
00:02:35.446 --> 00:02:36.427
In a one-on-one.

81
00:02:36.707 --> 00:02:39.715
What's the most important thing I should focus on this quarter?

82
00:02:39.995 --> 00:02:41.681
What's one thing I could do better?

83
00:02:41.961 --> 00:02:43.859
What does the next level look like for me?

84
00:02:45.209 --> 00:02:46.383
With a stakeholder.

85
00:02:46.663 --> 00:02:48.348
What would success look like for you?

86
00:02:48.628 --> 00:02:51.807
If we can only deliver one of these, which matters most?

87
00:02:52.087 --> 00:02:53.985
What happens if this is a week late?

88
00:02:55.335 --> 00:02:56.253
Part 4.

89
00:02:56.533 --> 00:02:59.455
How to ask a stupid question without feeling stupid.

90
00:03:00.705 --> 00:03:03.777
Use the context + attempt + question pattern:

91
00:03:05.127 --> 00:03:06.066
You could say:

92
00:03:06.346 --> 00:03:08.693
I'm new to the payments module (context).

93
00:03:08.973 --> 00:03:15.479
I read the README and traced the refund() call (attempt), but I can't see where the ledger entry is created.

94
00:03:15.759 --> 00:03:17.637
Can you point me in the right direction?

95
00:03:17.917 --> 00:03:18.791
(question).

96
00:03:20.141 --> 00:03:23.192
It shows you put in effort first, and people respect that.

97
00:03:24.542 --> 00:03:25.502
Part 5.

98
00:03:25.782 --> 00:03:27.489
Listening blockers to avoid.

99
00:03:28.739 --> 00:03:31.213
Interrupting to show you already know the answer.

100
00:03:31.493 --> 00:03:34.715
Solving before the person has finished describing the problem.

101
00:03:34.995 --> 00:03:37.576
Checking your phone or Slack mid-conversation.

102
00:03:37.856 --> 00:03:39.413
That's nothing, once I had...

103
00:03:39.693 --> 00:03:41.443
(turning their story into yours).

104
00:03:41.723 --> 00:03:45.051
Filling every silence; give people 3 seconds to think.

105
00:03:46.401 --> 00:03:47.339
Part 6.

106
00:03:47.619 --> 00:03:49.006
Practice conversation:

107
00:03:49.286 --> 00:03:50.929
Clarifying a vague request.

108
00:03:52.179 --> 00:03:54.205
Here is how the conversation goes.

109
00:03:54.485 --> 00:03:56.683
We need the report page to be faster.

110
00:03:56.963 --> 00:03:58.456
Users are complaining.

111
00:03:58.736 --> 00:03:59.632
Got it.

112
00:03:59.912 --> 00:04:05.523
When you say faster, which part feels slow: opening the page, or downloading the report?

113
00:04:05.803 --> 00:04:07.424
Mostly opening the page.

114
00:04:07.704 --> 00:04:09.048
It takes ages.

115
00:04:09.328 --> 00:04:10.565
Roughly how long?

116
00:04:10.845 --> 00:04:12.403
Five seconds, ten?

117
00:04:12.683 --> 00:04:14.965
Around twelve seconds for big customers.

118
00:04:16.315 --> 00:04:17.915
The conversation continues.

119
00:04:18.195 --> 00:04:23.187
So the goal is under three seconds for opening the page, for our biggest customers.

120
00:04:23.467 --> 00:04:24.641
Is that right?

121
00:04:24.921 --> 00:04:25.902
Exactly.

122
00:04:26.182 --> 00:04:29.254
And is this needed for this release, or next month?

123
00:04:29.534 --> 00:04:30.921
Next month is fine.

124
00:04:32.271 --> 00:04:33.807
That's the end of this lesson.

125
00:04:34.087 --> 00:04:38.759
Pick one script from it, say it out loud three times, and use it for real today.

126
00:04:39.039 --> 00:04:45.140
For extra practice, paste the Master Prompt into an AI assistant and role-play the conversation.

